/*
	RMSTEST 8 — is terrain_to_place_on checked per member under TIGHT grouping?

	This is the sharper version of your suspicion, because it could reframe the
	model rather than just add an exception. If the flood-fill checks TILE
	VALIDITY (terrain, occupancy) per member while skipping DISTANCE-based
	avoidances, then "tight = anchor only" is wrong as stated, and the real rule
	is:

	    anchor must satisfy everything;
	    fill members must satisfy tile-level validity but not distance rules.

	That model would also explain guide:2102's "if the number_of_objects exceeds
	the available number of tiles, then a perfect square worth of objects will be
	filled" — a cap that only makes sense if the fill is aware of which tiles it
	may use.

	GEOMETRY — deterministic, no random land placement.
	  A 15x15 GRASS clearing at land_position 50 50 (tile 60,60 on a 120 map).
	  Player lands are DESERT and pushed to circle_radius 45, so the ONLY grass
	  on the map is that clearing — which means create_terrain DIRT with
	  base_terrain GRASS has nowhere else it can go.
	  DIRT is given number_of_tiles 9 in a single clump, so roughly a 3x3 patch
	  sits somewhere inside the 15x15 grass square.
	  The gold then asks for 25 objects with terrain_to_place_on DIRT — four
	  times more objects than there are dirt tiles.

	READ IT LIKE THIS
	  Gold appears on GRASS as well as dirt, roughly 25 of them, spilling out
	  around the dirt patch
	      -> terrain_to_place_on is checked ONLY on the anchor. The fill is
	         genuinely unchecked, "tight = anchor only" stands as a uniform rule,
	         and Sec.6.6 can state it simply.
	  Gold appears ONLY on the dirt tiles, about 9 of them, and none on grass
	      -> terrain IS checked per member. The refined two-tier model above is
	         the right one, and Sec.6.6 must distinguish tile-validity
	         constraints from distance constraints instead of treating tight
	         grouping as one rule.
	  Gold count is between those, or on dirt plus exactly one ring of grass
	      -> note it precisely; a partial spill would suggest the fill checks
	         terrain but relaxes when it runs out, which is a third behaviour
	         neither the guide nor the spec describes.

	The count matters as much as the position here, so if you can, say roughly
	how many gold piles appear as well as what they are standing on. Dirt and
	grass are easy to tell apart, which is why this uses terrain rather than a
	layer.

	STONE is the CONTROL: same land, no terrain restriction, must appear.

	Both create_objects carry set_gaia_object_only, since Test 3 confirmed a
	frame-referenced non-ownable object without it places nothing.

	RUN ON: Tiny, 2 players. One clearing at map centre, one sample per seed.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
base_terrain FOREST

create_player_lands
{
	terrain_type DESERT
	base_size 3
	number_of_tiles 1
	circle_radius 45 0
}

create_land
{
	terrain_type GRASS
	land_position 50 50
	base_size 7
	number_of_tiles 1
	land_id 5
	zone 7
}

<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

create_terrain DIRT
{
	base_terrain GRASS
	number_of_tiles 9
	number_of_clumps 1
}

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

/* THE TEST: 25 objects restricted to a ~9-tile terrain patch. */
create_object GOLD
{
	number_of_objects 25
	set_tight_grouping
	set_gaia_object_only
	place_on_specific_land_id 5
	terrain_to_place_on DIRT
}

/* CONTROL: same land, no terrain restriction. Must appear. */
create_object STONE
{
	number_of_objects 1
	set_gaia_object_only
	place_on_specific_land_id 5
}
